How do you beat a 235B model with 32B parameters on long-document understanding? You teach it to reason during training and internalize it at inference. Meet @LightOnIO OriOn-Qwen-SR1, which is open source.

This model reasons over full documents and executes it implicitly at inference, compressing reasoning into the model's weights without verbose output or added latency. It achieves state-of-the-art results on MMLongBenchDoc, outperforming Qwen3 VL with seven times fewer parameters.

@LightOnIO: "Introducing New SOTA Long Context VLM. Reasoning starts at reading. Kudos to @further_ai for this new milestone!"

@LightOnIO is one of the last hopes for the information retrieval community. I still believe retrieval is not dead; it has potential beyond mere markdown management for agentic memories. They open-sourced two retrievers, LateOn and DenseOn, both with 149M parameters, under Apache 2.0, trained on identical data with different architectures.

The interesting finding is what happens when you actually test generalization. Dense retrieval squashes each document into one vector at index time, comparing it against the query vector. This is fast but lossy, as it throws away everything not compressed. Late interaction (ColBERT-style) keeps a vector per token, matching token-to-token at query time, which preserves way more information but requires more compute.

The BEIR benchmark is the standard, but the problem is that training corpora overlap with evaluation corpora, so scores measure familiarity as much as retrieval quality. LightOn rebuilt the evaluation by stripping every document from BEIR that appeared in their training data. Dense models dropped in rank under decontamination, while ColBERT models held or climbed, with an average delta of +2.01 for dense and +3.55 for multi-vector. This shows that in niche, internal, or domain-specific corpora that the model has never seen, compressed dense vectors fall apart while late interaction continues to perform well.

